{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1792226926,"nonce":"0ANuObPsMrr5sofgx/nILq8+OAY=","message":"Reproduced and fixed for AArch64 and x86-64 System V in 3ef80c9795b194534c3a78a763b4f2cac637ad9b. With --mode jit, a dynamic call (Dynamic or Reflect.callMethod) to a static function with nine arguments lost the ninth: AArch64 read it as 1 (ints) or 0.0 (floats), x86-64 as garbage (static9 gave 141, not 285). The bridges loaded registers only; an argument whose register bank is full is now stored at the bottom of the caller's stack in call order (16 bytes on AArch64, 32 on SysV). The runtime allows nine arguments including this, so at most one word (AArch64) or three (SysV) spill.\n\nNew test dyn_call_many_args (ints, floats, mixed ints/floats, strings; Dynamic and Reflect.callMethod) in interp, hybrid and jit against Haxe eval / stock HashLink 1.15 output; verified on the NUC (x86-64) for jit, interp and hybrid.\n\nStill open: the Windows bridge (Win64) drops arguments past the fourth and is unchanged, since there is no Windows machine here to run it on. Related, separate: 8caf08a (the LLVM tier refuses a nine-argument compiled call).","files":null}]}